---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="fabrication — tiny soft computer"
  description="Materials and techniques for building your tiny soft computer's enclosure."
  active="fabrication"
>

  <section class="hero" style="padding-top: 2.5rem;">
    <p class="kicker">fabrication</p>
    <h1>the form is yours to decide</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      a computer doesn't have to look or behave the way you expect, this
      is where that idea becomes something you hold. we bring soft
      materials, you're also welcome to bring your own.
    </p>
    <img
      class="step-image"
      src="/images/enclosure-example.png"
      alt="A finished tiny soft computer enclosure, hand-knit in soft grey yarn, with the eInk screen and a button on an alligator clip visible through the knit fabric."
    >
    <p class="note" style="margin-top: -0.6rem;">
      a finished example, hand-knit by Alanna. yours doesn't have to
      look anything like this, it's just one answer.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <span class="tag">provided</span>
    <h2>materials we bring</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li>felt and fabric scraps</li>
      <li>cardboard</li>
      <li>craft supplies (glue gun, scissors, xacto knives)</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      section still being written, full technique and step-by-step notes
      coming soon.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <span class="tag">bring your own</span>
    <h2>repurposed items</h2>
    <p>
      an old tin, a cassette case, packaging, fabric scraps, anything
      that already means something to you is a good starting point for a
      mount or enclosure.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>the only real constraints</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li>it needs to fit around the electronics' footprint</li>
      <li>the screen needs to stay visible</li>
      <li>the button needs to stay reachable</li>
    </ul>
    <p>everything else is open.</p>
  </section>

</Layout>